From 3b79c6a25f273da1549784c345dcabfd12136ae4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agents-factory-hubpatch <agents-factory-hubpatch@exe.xyz>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:29:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(bd): a contended lock names its holder, with visible
 bounded waits and content-free diagnostics (agents-elu6)

`flock -w 300 <lock> <cmd>` waits in silence and then exits 1 with NO output at all (measured against
util-linux 2.39.3). Measured cost of that on 2026-10-13: a COMPLETE cross-family review could not record
its verdict for more than four minutes because another lane's `bd dolt push` held this VM's shared lock
for the whole network round-trip of its child `git push --force-with-lease refs/dolt/data` - so a lane
holding a finished result produced no output, looked IDLE, and the failure carried no reason. It is the
zombie-lane and silent-spawn failure shape one layer down, and the verdict it delays is the artefact that
authorises a landing.

The holder now names itself: while it holds the lock it writes one record line into the lock FILE (a lock
object, never a data file - flock's lock is on the fd/inode, so writing the record does not disturb it)
and clears it before releasing. A waiter that cannot take the lock reports the holder's lane, role, pid,
operation and how long it has been held, plus the holder's descendant processes - which is where a push
actually spends its minutes. The wait keeps its 300s of patience (FLEET_BD_LOCK_WAIT overrides) but is
reported on first contention and at most every FLEET_BD_LOCK_REPORT seconds, and on expiry it fails FAST
with EX_TEMPFAIL (75), holder named, having changed nothing. The lock fd is closed FOR THE bd PAYLOAD
ONLY, so a child bd leaves behind cannot keep the fleet locked after bd exits.

A DIAGNOSTIC MUST NOT NEED THE CONTENT IT IS DIAGNOSING (agents-zoch), IN EVERY FIELD AND EVERY POSITION.
The first version of this patch stored and reported the holder's full argv, and rev-elu6 defeated it by
DRIVING it rather than reading it: `bd comment issue-x SENTINEL-PRIVATE-PAYLOAD` put that text into the
shared lock file AND into the waiting lane's stderr. Both surfaces belong to other people. Since a filter
must anticipate the payload while a vocabulary cannot express one, every reported value is now a
vocabulary word (bd operations, dolt operations, process names, roles), a validated integer (pid,
timestamp), or a lane name the fleet's OWN REGISTRY on this VM vouches for; anything else is the literal
`unrecognised` / `other` / `unregistered` / `unknown`. Four positions are covered and the last three were
found by attacking this patch: the operation, the subcommand slot (freeform text), FLEET_LANE/FLEET_ROLE
(environment, so the holder copies what an actor set - measured leaking before this revision), and the
RECORD ITSELF, which is a 0644 file any process can write - so the waiter re-validates every field it
reads and a planted record with arbitrary text in every field now reports UNNAMED. A descendant's argv
(`ps -o args=`) is gone too: pid plus a vocabulary process name only.

tests/bd-lock-holder.test.sh pins all of it hermetically: a stub $HOME/.local/bin/bd, a throwaway home and
a throwaway lane registry, so it never contends with the fleet. 56 cases, 21 of them the attacks above as
regression tests. Against the pre-change shim the same file reports 27 passed / 29 failed, while the
existing tests/bd-shim.test.sh stays 15/15. Uncontended cost: +0.47ms per call (8.23 -> 8.70ms, best of 6
interleaved 40-call runs). Applies in both orders with the other pending bin/bd patch
(fleet-worktree-beads-guard.patch); the composed shim passes all three suites.

Release-note: none (fleet tooling; no user-facing surface)

--- P1 FIX (found by review, on the revision above): THE LOCK MUST BE HELD BY THE WORK, NOT BY THIS SHELL ---

The revision above closed the lock fd for the bd child, and a reviewer's probe found what that costs:
SIGKILL the shim while bd is STILL WRITING and the lock is released, so a second bd runs CONCURRENTLY with
the first. Measured here, both directions:

  SIGKILL the shim while bd is writing, then run a second call:
    as deployed (flock(1)'s child inherits the fd):  waits 5s for the work, then runs  -> serialised
    the revision above (fd closed for the child):    runs IMMEDIATELY, 0s, rc=0        -> BYPASS, two writers
    this revision (bd inherits):                     refused, rc=75 with a 1s bound,
                                                     while the first bd was STILL WRITING

Killing a shim is normal operation in this fleet (bounded gates, the reaper, lane restarts), so this was a
bypass we would have hit rather than an exotic one - and it defeated the entire point of the lock. bd now
INHERITS the lock fd, so the lock outlives the shim for as long as the WORK lasts. The fd close was a fix
for a real but lesser problem (a stray descendant holding the lock after bd exits), and the two failure
directions are not symmetric: OVER-holding delays other lanes, while UNDER-holding - the lock released
while bd writes - breaks the one invariant this shim exists for. The inheritance stays and the over-hold is
REPORTED rather than prevented: a record whose pid is gone while the lock is still held is exactly that
case, and the waiter says the record is stale and the lock is still held. Nothing on the waiting side could
have fixed this: while the shim is dead and bd is writing, the lock is FREE, so a second call acquires it
without ever seeing contention - correctness comes from the fd.

Case 9 of tests/bd-lock-holder.test.sh pins it, and it is the only case that distinguishes this revision
from the one it replaces: against the fd-closing revision exactly 9a and 9b fail (54 passed / 2 failed),
and against the shim as deployed the suite is 24 passed / 32 failed.

Release-note: none (fleet tooling; no user-facing surface)

--- ALSO IN THIS REVISION: the glob/path property is now PINNED, outcome and order (coord's call) ---

Reviews asked whether the character-class check before the registry lookup is tested, and it was not - only
argued. Case 15 now does both, and measuring it corrected the mechanism I had described: bash does NOT
glob-expand inside `[ -e "..." ]`, so the property does not rest on globbing at all. It rests on two things
the character class stops regardless of quoting: `..` is resolved by the KERNEL inside an otherwise-quoted
path, so `../../etc/passwd` would be vouched for by a file OUTSIDE the registry; and `*` is a legal filename
character, so `impl-*` would be vouched for by a file literally named `impl-*.model`. Case 15 plants both
files, so its four outcome assertions discriminate the ORDER rather than merely agreeing with it, and 15d
asserts the order structurally by reading the function's CODE (not its comments - this function's own
docstring names ~/.fleet/lanes/<name>.model, and the first draft of 15d failed on that prose).

Evidence the pin is load-bearing: against a REORDERED copy of the shim (lookup first) case 15 goes red in all
nine of its places - the four outcome pairs plus 15d - while a suite that only checked outcomes against an
unplanted registry would have passed that mutation. Suite is 66/66 here, 28/38 against the shim as deployed,
64/2 against the fd-closing revision (the two are 9a/9b).

--- P2 FIX (agents-6e77, found by review): THE LANE FIELD IS GONE, because nothing can vouch for it ---

The reviewer's sentence: the lane registry is 0755 exedev-owned and IS NOT AN INDEPENDENT TRUST BOUNDARY. The
previous revision accepted a lane name only when ~/.fleet/lanes/<name>.model existed - but EVERY LANE CAN
WRITE THAT DIRECTORY, so a same-uid caller creates the file, sets FLEET_LANE, and chosen text reaches the
shared record and from there another lane's stderr. That is the agents-zoch class again, one layer out: the
name was validated against a thing the attacker can write.

The repair is NOT to validate harder against a writable directory (that polishes the bypass) but to REMOVE THE
FIELD. On a shared-uid VM nothing proves a name: the registry is writable, FLEET_LANE is environment, and a
process names itself. So fleet_bd_lock_lane is deleted, the record carries pid/role/since/cmd, and the report
describes the holder by role (closed vocabulary), pid and age (integers), the operation (closed vocabulary)
and its descendant process names (same vocabulary). Anything an attacker writes into the shared record is now
IGNORED rather than filtered. The lane field was the only field that could carry attacker-chosen text -
verified by inspecting every other one: pid/since are integers, role and cmd are closed vocabularies, and
comm is already filtered to a vocabulary (so a descendant that renames its executable reports as 'other',
which is now case 15e).

Red first: the new case 15 is the reviewer's probe - create the registry file, set FLEET_LANE, and check the
record AND another lane's stderr - and against the revision before this one it FAILS in exactly those
assertions (15a/15b/15c of 6 failures, 57 passed), while the deployed shim gives 32 passed / 31 failed. Also
in this revision: the glob/order property pinned last round is MOOT, because the field it protected no longer
exists - removing the field answers that class more strongly than a pin could, and it is stated in the
write-up rather than silently dropped. The P1 fix is unaffected: the SIGKILL probe still reports rc=75 with
the first bd still writing. Suite 63/63.

--- DOCS: the threat-model boundary is now stated, not inferred (coord's request) ---

Coord asked for the threat model to be stated plainly rather than left for a reader to infer, since three
successive reviews found the same class in three successive positions. New section "What this patch promises,
and where the boundary is": the property (no lane's text reaches a shared file or another lane's log; every
value is a closed vocabulary or a validated integer, re-validated on read), the BOUNDARY (the lock file is
0644 and the lane registry 0755 - both writable by the single unix user every lane runs as, so the guarantee is
NOT that the record is authentic, only that nothing a same-uid writer puts there is ever printed; a lane cannot
prove its identity to another lane on this VM, and naming needs a trust boundary the fleet controls), and what
is explicitly NOT promised (the wait is not shortened; flock -w polls rather than queues, so the fairness
defect is agents-nbka, because a timeout cannot be tuned into a queue).

DOCS ONLY - no behaviour change. Verified below: the applied bin/bd and tests/bd-lock-holder.test.sh are
byte-identical to the copies the reviewers saw for 602bec9, so the P1 verdict (lock inheritance) and the
reader-side revalidation conclusions carry over unchanged; only the write-up differs.

--- DOCS: what the defect COSTS, measured (coord's request) ---

Coord: the lock is now a lane-visible outage rather than a nuisance, and the write-up should say what it costs
rather than only what it violates. New section "What NOT having this costs, measured on 2026-10-13": four lanes'
bead writes timing out in one landing window (a refused write is work that does not exist for the fleet); one
finding's sentence that had to be recorded by ANOTHER actor to survive; multiple 300s waits on the git
pre-commit hook (agents-vqwg, since a commit takes the same lock); and this lane's direct starvation
measurement (4 calls refused at 90s = 360s with zero acquisitions while the lock was continuously served, then
one 240s acquisition running five operations nested). Plus the costs OF the patch for symmetry: one extra open
fd per call, one short record line, +0.47ms uncontended, and the loss of the holder's lane name - and what it
does NOT do: shorten a wait or make the lock fair (agents-nbka); it makes the wait visible, nameable, bounded
and survivable.

DOCS ONLY again, verified: applied bin/bd and tests/bd-lock-holder.test.sh are byte-identical to the copies the
reviewers saw for 602bec9 (cmp), so every behaviour conclusion carries over and only the write-up differs.

--- DOCS: the rollout story, measured on the real lock (coord's request) ---

Coord: the UNNAMED-holder case is the rollout story and belongs in the write-up, because "patched waiter,
unpatched holder" is what the fleet will actually run for a while. New section "The rollout story": the live
transcripts from the real lock while writing that section (reports at 0m0s and 0m30s, holder UNNAMED because a
PRE-PATCH bd held it, then the call ran; and a second attempt that hit its 1m30s bound and changed nothing),
plus a table of the three states - patched/patched names role+pid+operation+descendants; pre-patch holder with
a patched waiter degrades to UNNAMED with the reason rather than inventing one; and a pre-patch waiter prints
nothing at all for up to 300s, which is the state this patch removes. Also stated: the record is advisory
throughout (the lock is the fd, so a pre-patch holder is safely serialised against a patched waiter), and an
empty lock file beside an active lock means "a holder whose record I cannot read", not "nobody is here".

DOCS ONLY, verified: applied bin/bd and tests/ byte-identical to the reviewers' copies for 602bec9 (cmp).
