+# lane registry vouched for - but THAT REGISTRY IS WRITABLE BY EVERY LANE, so it is not a trust boundary, and
+# a same-uid caller could put chosen text into the shared record and from there into another lane's stderr.
+# On a shared-uid VM nothing proves a name: the registry is writable, FLEET_LANE is environment, and a process
+# names itself. So every field here is a CLOSED vocabulary (role, operation, process names) or an INTEGER
+# (pid, age), and nothing an attacker writes into the shared file can carry text. Cases 13-15 attack that
+# from the environment, from a planted record, and through a registry file a same-uid caller creates.
+
+# stub real bd: echo its argv, optionally spawn a git-shaped child and sleep (the shape of the real latency)
+cat > "$T/.local/bin/bd" <<'STUB'
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+echo "REAL-BD:$*"
+if [ "${STUB_SECRET_BIN:-0}" -gt 0 ]; then
+  "$SECRET_BIN" & wait $!          # a child whose EXECUTABLE NAME is attacker-chosen
+fi
